quotes.tex:
For distinct proper nontrivial $h,g$, partition $\X$ by membership: $A=\supp(h)\cap\supp(g)$, $B=\supp(h)\setminus\supp(g)$, $C=\supp(g)\setminus\supp(h)$, $D=\X\setminus(\supp(h)\cup\supp(g))$.
Then
\begin{align*}
   g\text{ is not eliminable from }h
   \;\iff\;
   (A\ne\varnothing\Rightarrow D\ne\varnothing)\;\wedge\;
   (B\ne\varnothing\Rightarrow C\ne\varnothing).
\end{align*}
Equivalently, $g$ is not eliminable from $h$ in exactly three regimes: \textnormal{(N1)} $\supp(h)\subsetneq\supp(g)$ (\emph{superset}); \textnormal{(N2)} incomparable and disjoint (\emph{disjoint}); \textnormal{(N3)} incomparable, intersecting, and non-covering (\emph{non-covering}).

terms.tex:
Writing $\F\Subset\Hyp$ for ``$\F$ is a finite subset of $\Hyp$'', the \emph{confusability complex} is
\begin{align*}
   \Conf(\Hyp):=\{\F\Subset\Hyp:\F\ne\varnothing\text{ and admits a shared contrastive presentation}\},
\end{align*}
an abstract simplicial complex (downward closed under nonempty subsets); we adopt the convention that the empty face is excluded.
